🔴 Ways to BREAK Singleton Pattern
________________________________________
1️⃣ Break using Reflection (MOST COMMON)
❌ Problem
Private constructor can be accessed via reflection.
Example
Constructor<JudgeAnalytics> constructor =
        JudgeAnalytics.class.getDeclaredConstructor();
constructor.setAccessible(true);

JudgeAnalytics obj1 = JudgeAnalytics.getInstance();
JudgeAnalytics obj2 = constructor.newInstance();

System.out.println(obj1 == obj2); // false ❌
🔥 Singleton is broken
Two different objects created.
________________________________________
✅ Fix: Protect constructor
private JudgeAnalytics() {
    if (Holder.instance != null) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Use getInstance()");
    }
}
________________________________________
2️⃣ Break using Serialization
❌ Problem
During deserialization, JVM creates a new object.
ObjectOutputStream oos = new ObjectOutputStream(new FileOutputStream("file"));
oos.writeObject(instance);

ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream("file"));
JudgeAnalytics obj2 = (JudgeAnalytics) ois.readObject();
❌ New instance created.
________________________________________
✅ Fix: readResolve()
protected Object readResolve() {
    return getInstance();
}
This forces JVM to return the same instance.
________________________________________
3️⃣ Break using Cloning
❌ Problem
If class implements Cloneable, clone creates new object.
JudgeAnalytics clone = (JudgeAnalytics) instance.clone();
________________________________________
✅ Fix: Block cloning
@Override
protected Object clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException {
    throw new CloneNotSupportedException();
}
________________________________________
4️⃣ Break using Multithreading (bad lazy init)
❌ Problem
if (instance == null) {
    instance = new JudgeAnalytics();
}
Two threads can enter at same time.
________________________________________
✅ Fix
•	synchronized
•	volatile + double checked locking
•	Bill Pugh
•	Enum Singleton
